Residents of Macedonia vote in referendum on whether to change the country's name
On Sunday, the people of Macedonia point in a referendum on the question of whether to change its name to the “Republic of North Macedonia,” a move that will solve a decades-long dispute with Greece that prevented it from joining the European Union and NATO.
Greece, in which there is a province called Macedonia, claims that its neighbor’s name from the north is a claim of Greece’s territory and vetoed its accession to NATO and the European Union.
The two countries have signed a deal based on the proposed new name in June, but nationalist opponents argue that the change will harm the ethnic identity of the Slavic majority population in Macedonia.
President George Ivanov said he wouldn’t vote in the referendum and a boycott campaign questioned the question of whether the amount of voters would stand at a minimum of 50 percent it takes for the referendum to be valid.
“Are you in favor of joining NATO and the European Union upon acceptance of the agreement with Greece.”
Those who support the name change, including Prime Minister Zoran Zayev, claim that this is a price worth paying for Macedonia, which is one of the countries created from the dissolution of Yugoslavia, will be able to join bodies such as the European Union and NATO.
“I’ve come today to vote the country’s future for young people in Macedonia so they can live freedom under the umbrella of the European Union, because that means a protected life for all of us,” said 79-year-old Olivera Georgizbakka in Skopje.
Although the referendum is not legally obligatory, enough parliamentarians have said that they will comply with the results of the vote to make it.
In order to change the name, a two-thirds majority in Parliament will be required.
The State Elections Committee said there were no irregularities at 13:00.
However, the percentage of voters was only 16 percent, compared to 34 percent in the last parliamentary elections in 2016, in which 66 percent of eligible voters came to vote.
