Macedonian Prime Minister Zoran Zayev, his his wife Zurika and his son Düshko deplay their ballot in Stromicia, Macedonia, on September 30, 2018, in Macedonña's referendum to change the country's name, which will open the door to join NATO and the European Union.
In the front of the House of Parliament in Skopje, Vladimør Kavarardkov, prepared a small stage and chairs in front of tents set by those boycotting the referendum.
“We’re for NATO and the European Union, but we’d want to join an uptight head, not through the entry of the servants,” said Kawardarkov.
“We’re a poor country, but we have respect.
If they don’t want to take us as Macedonia, we can appeal to others, such as China and Russia, and become part of Euro-Essian unification.”
Prime Minister Zayev said that NATO companies will bring very necessary investments to Macedonia, where the unemployment rate exceeds 20 percent.
“I believe that the vast majority will be in favor because more than 80 percent of our citizens support the European Union and NATO,” Zayev said after voting.
He said a positive result would be “security for our future.”
A survey published on Monday by the Macedonia Policy Research Institute showed that between 30 and 43% of voters will participate in the referendum – less than the percentage of participation required.
Another referendum, edited by the Macedonian television network Telma TV, found that 57 of respondents plan to vote on Sunday.
Of these, 70 percent said they’d vote “yes.”
For the referendum to succeed, the percentage of voters must be at least 50 percent and another.
