The Labour Party is no longer a pluralistic place.
She's into tolerant of those expressing their opinions
Which Momentum activists in my local party branch voted to censor me, it wasn’t quite a surprise.
After all, I’m the last in a series of members of the Libor that they were told they weren’t wanted – and only because we expressed our opinion.
My parliamentary colleague, Joan Ryan, received the same treatment because she vehemented against anti-anti-Semitism.
In my case, the decision to censor me was criticized for not agreeing with Jeremy Corbin.
Regarding the importance of responsible economic policies, for national security, to Europe – to most of the irony, issues similar to those for which Jeremi did not agree with previous party leaders.
“We want the meeting of East Nottingham’s Liber party on Friday, “We we want the meeting to be inclusive and productive.”
For most of my eight years as a member of a local parliament from the Labour Party, the meetings of the General Committee on Fridays eve were exactly like that.
Trust-to-day today, it’s no tone of many of the meetings and the “coorrier, gentler” politics has long been forgotten, if it has ever even begun.
It turns out more and more that the Labour Party has no tolerance for different views, and every opinion is judged according to its being accepted by the party’s leadership.
This situation began shortly after Jeremy was elected head of the party, when colleagues in the past I thought we were sharing a similar political outlook, began expecting him to make a U-turn and take positions that I would never have agreed to – whether about national security or one European market.
Every time I speak in public – and it doesn’t really matter what I’m saying – Immediately comes an abusive fluency of rebuke on social media with calls for elimination, condemning the politics of the center and calls that I don’t have to be in the Labour Party.
And it's not just my experience.
In fact, I know I'm luckier than many of my colleagues, because the comments directed at me tend to be political.
“I’m amazed by the professionalism and determination of those colleagues who stand every day in the face of a flux of racist abuse but never retreat.
One of the most disappointing aspects of this era in politics is how levels of abuse have become commonplace.
Jeremy Corbin argued last week that the Labour Party should cultivate a culture of tolerance.
The reality is that we are already the same pluralistic place, and with any “non-confidence” decision or a change in the rules of choice, the party is becoming narrower.
Over the past two years many have advised me to keep my mouth shut, don’t be so loud and then “I’ll be okay.”
But that’s not why I went into politics.
Since I joined the Labour Party 32 years ago as a school student, it has been shaken by the neglect of the Teacher government that caused my public school class to almost disintegrate, I’ve searched for how to fight better social services for those who need them the most—whether as a member of a local council or a government minister.
"I've never hidden my political views, including in the last election.
No person in East Nottingham could in any way be confused about my politic attitudes and issues of disagreement with the current leadership.
For those who promoted the decision on Friday, all I have to say is that while the state is crawling towards Brexit that will harm our families, business and public service, I don’t understand the desire to waste time and energy on my loyalty to the Labour leader.
But in truth, the one message I have is not for the momentum movement in Nottingham, but to my constituent, whether they are members of the Labour Party or not: I am proud to serve you and I promise that no amount of impeachment threats or political utilitarianism will deter me in a way that I believe best serves the interests of all of you.
Chris Leslie is an MP from East Nottingham.
