'The Sea is Ours': A Bolivia Who Can't Find a Marine Hope the Tribue Will Open a Path to the Pacific
Sailors patrol the Naviry Headquarters with a cloak of ships in La Paz.
Public buildings swewing a blue-sea flag.
Marine bases from Lake Titicaca to the Amazon bear the slogan: “The sea is ours by merit.
“It’s our duty to sue back.”
All over Bulibia that there is no way out to the sea, the memory of a lost coastline for Chile in a bloody conflict on resources in the 19th century is still alive—as well as the longing to sail again in the Pacific Ocean.
These hopes are perhaps at their peak for decades, while Bolivia is awaiting an International Court of Justice’s decision on October 1 after five years of hearings.
“Bulivia has momentum, the spirit of unity and tranquility, and of course it expects from a positive attitude to come,” said Roberto Calazilla, a Bolivian diplomat.
Many Bolivians will be nurtured by the International Court’s decision on large screens across the country, in the hope that the Heague Tribunal will decide in favor of Bolivia’s demand that after decades of fragmented talks, Chile will be required to negotiate a sovereign approach to the sea to Bolivia.
Bolivia’s charsimatic native president, Evo Morales, facing a controversial struggle for re-election next year, hangs a lot in the decision on Monday.
“We’re very close to go back to the Pacific,” he promised at the end of August.
But commentators believe that there is not much a chance that the court will decide for Bolivia – and that even if it decides in her favor, it won’t change much.
The U.N. court that sits in The Hague has no authority to grant Chile territory, noting that it would not determine the consequences of possible talks.
The fact that the decision of the International Tribual came only six weeks after hearing the final arguments indicates that the case “was not complex”, but Paz Zarata, a Chilean expert in international law.
Instead of promoting Bolivia’s claims, it is even possible that the past four years have weakened them.
“The issue of access to the sea has been taken by the current government in Bolivia,” Zarata said.
Morales' war rhetoric wiped out every remnant of goodwill on the part of Chile, according to her.
At some point, Bolivia and Chile will continue to talk, but it’s going to be very difficult to have discussions after that.
The twive countries have not exchanged ambassadors since 162.
The previous president, Eduardo Rodriguez and Leza, Bolivia’s representative in The Hague, rejected the idea that the court’s decision was unusually rapid.
On Monday, Bolivia will receive a “great opportunity to open a new era in relations with Chile” and the chance of “put an end to 139 years of controversy for both sides,” he said.
Klazadilla has also denied that Morales—he’s still one of Latin America’s most popular president—uses the marine issue as a political lever.
“Bolivia will never renounce the right to access the Pacific,” he added.
“Stop is an opportunity to see that we have to overcome the past.”
