John Edward: Essential Language Skills for Citizens of the World
Independent schools in Scotland maintain a record of academic excellence, and this continues in 2018 with an additional cluster of outstanding test results, which is reinforced by individual and group success in sports, art, music and other areas of activity in the community.
With over £30,000 students across Scotland, these schools, represented by the Scottish Independent Schools (SCIS), are striving to provide the best level of service for students and parents.
Independent schools strive to prepare their students for an additional and higher education, for a career they will choose and their place as the citizens of the world.
As an education sector that is able to plan and assimilate a personalized curriculum, we discover that modern languages continue to gain popularity as a sought-after field of study in school.
Nelson Mandela said, “If you’re talking to a person in a language he understands, it gets into his head.
“If you’re talking to a man in his own language, it gets into his heart.”
It’s an important reminder that we can’t just rely on English when we want to build relationships and trust with people from other countries.
From the results of the latest tests this year, you can see that languages are at the top of tables with the highest success rates in independent schools.
A total rate of 68 percent of students who have studied foreign languages have scored a high A score.
The data collected from the 74 SCIS schools showed that 72 percent of the students scored a high A score in Mandarin, as well as 72 percent of those who studied German, 69 percent of those who studied French and 63 percent of those who studied Spanish.
These data show that independent schools in Scotland support foreign languages as essential skills that children and teenagers will undoubtedly need them in the future.
Today the field of languages as an electoral subject is considered at the same level as STEM (Science, Technology, Engineering and Mathematics) in the curricula of independent schools and elsewhere.
A survey conducted in 2014 by the British Council on Employment and Skills found that, among the reasons employers noted Kodi to fill vacancies, 17% were attributed to a lack of language skills.
Therefore, to an increasing degree, language skills are becoming an essential need to prepare students for careers in the future.
In view of the increase in language requirements for future job opportunities, these qualifications are essential in the world of globalization.
It doesn’t matter in what career a person chooses, if he has learned a second language, he will have a significant advantage in the future thanks to this ability that lasts a lifetime.
The ability to communicate directly with people from foreign countries will automatically stay a person in an advantageous position on competitors.
According to a YouGov survey that included more than 4,000 adults in the United Kingdom in 2013, 75 percent could not despensible foreign languist for having a conversation, and French was the only language in which there was a double-sferting number of speakers, 15 percent.
That’s why it’s important to invest today in teaching languages for children.
Knowledge in many languages, especially those of developing countries, will give the children a better chance of finding significant employment.
In Scotland, each school will be different about the languages that are taught in it.
Some schools will focus on the more classic modern languages, while others will learn languages that are considered the most important for the United Kingdom looking forward to the twentieth years of the 21st twentieth, such as Manderin and Japanese.
No matter the child’s area of interest, there will always be a variety of languages to choose from him in independent schools, with a team of teachers who specialize in the field.
Independent schools in Scotland are committed to provide a learning environment that will prepare children and equip them with the skills it takes to succeed, at any expected future.
It is impossible to deniny today, in a global business environment that languages continue to be of vital importance to the future of the country, so this fact should be expressed in education.
Indeed, it is necessary to see in modern languages “International Communication Skills”.
Independent schools will continue to offer these levels of choice, diversity and excellence for children of Scotland.
It's the Order of the Hour.
John Edward is the Head of the Scottish Council for Independent Schools
