Harvard Students Take a Course on How to Rest Enough
This year’s New Harvard University course teaches participants how to sleep more, in an attempt to fight the expanding macho culture of learning by staying up all night night with caffeine.
An academic study found that students at the number one university in the world often have no idea when it comes to the basic principles of how to take care of themselves.
Charles Cassler, a professor of sleep science at Harvard Medical School and an expert at Brigahm & Weimans Hospital, planned the course, which is believed to be the first of its kind in the U.S.
He was inspired to open the course after a lecture he gave about the impact of sleep deprivation on learning.
“At the end of the lecture, one girl came to me and said: ‚ Why do they tell me this just now, in my senior year?”
She said that no one had ever told her about the importance of sleep—what surprised me,” he told the Telegraph newspaper.
The course, which is delivered for the first time this year, explains to students the principles of how good sleeping habits help academic and athletic performance, as well as improving their overall condition.
Paul Bryera, a professor of psychiatry at Harvard Medical School and Senior Director of the University’s Health Services, said the university had decided to open the course after discovering that students had a severe sleep deprivation during the week.
An hour-long course includes a series of interactive tasks.
In one section, there's a picture of a dormitory room, where students click on coffee cups, curtains, sports clothes, and books - to get explanations about the effects of caffeine and light and how athletic performance is influenced by sleep deprivation, as well as the importance of going to sleep.
In another section, participants are told how persistent sleep deprivation may increase the risk of a heart attack, stroke, depression and cancer.
Afterwards, a map of the campus, if interactive symbols, encourages participants to think about their daily routine.
„We know it won’t change students’ behavior overnight.
But we believe they have the right to know – just as you have the right to know the effects of choosing cigarette smoke,” added Prof. Zeisler.
According to him, the culture of praisement for sleep-free night is still existing, adding that modern technology and increasing pressure on students are causing the problem of sleep deprivation.
Concern for adequate sleep, and for good sleep quality, should be the “secret weapon” of every student to combat stress, exhaustion and anxiety, he said—even to avoid weight gain, because sleep deprivation shifts the brain to starvation, so we’re hungry all the time.
Raymond Sue, a 19-year-old student from California who studies chemical and physical biology, helped Professor Zeisler shape the course, after attending one of his first-year classes at Harvard.
He said that the course enlightened him and motivated him to encourage a course for the entire campus.
The next step, he hopes, will ask all graduate students to complete a similar teaching program before joining the competitive institution.
Professor Zeisel recommended that students examine the possibility of aiming a clock that would ring when it is time to go to bed, and not just for an hour when it is necessary to wake up, and to be aware of the harmful effects of ‚ blue light that eliminated electronic screens and LED lighting, which can disturb the circadian rhythm and thus cause problems with falling asleep.
