A New Cancer Vaccination Can Teach the Immune System “Swee” Cancer Cells
A New Cancer Vaccine Can Teach the Immune System “Sweeing Cancer Cells and Eliminate Them”
Vaccination teaches the immune system to identify cancer cells as part of treatment
The method involves extracting immune cells from the patient, and changing the cells in the laboratory
Then the cells can “see” a protein that is common to many types of cancer and then they are injected back into the patient.
Experimental vaccination shows promising results in patients with a variety of cancers.
In one woman treated with a vaccine, which teaches the immune system to identify cancer cells, ovarian cancer disappears for more than 18 months.
The method includes extracting immune cells from the patient, changing the cells in the laboratory so that they can “see” a protein called HER2 which is common to many types of cancer, and then the injection of the cells returned to the patient.
Professor J Brezofsky of U.S. National Cancer Institute at Betsada, said: “Our results show that we have a very promising vaccine.”
HER2 „Motivates the development of a number of cancer, including breast, ovaries, visibility and coarse intestines, Prof. Brezofsky explained.
A similar approach of taking immune cells from patients and “teaching” them how to attack cancer cells has succeeded in addressing a type of blood cancer.
