HIV/AIDS: China Reports 14% Increase in New Cases
China announced a 14% increase in the number of its citizens living with HIV and AIDS.
More than 8,000 people with the disease, official health officials said.
Approximately 40,000 new cases were reported in the second half of 2018 alone.
The vast majority of new cases have been infected as a result of having sex, which is a change compared to the past.
To date, HIV has spread rapidly in some parts of China as a result of infected blood infusions.
But the number of people infected with HIV in this way has decreased to near-zero, according to Chinese officials at a conference in Yunnan Province.
However, from year to year, the number of people living with HIV and AIDS in China increased by 100,000 people.
Infection with HIV as a result of sexual intestrition is a burning problem in the LGBT community in China.
Homosexuality has ceased to law in China in 1997, but it is said that discrimination against LGBT people is widespread.
Because of the country’s conservative values, studies have estimated that 70-90 percent of men have sex with men will end up marrying women.
Many cases of infection of the disease are the result of non-use of adequate protective measures in these sexual intercourse.
Since 2003, the Chinese government has pledged universal access to HIV-drink medicine as part of an effort to deal with the problem.
