What is the World Citizens Festival and has it contributed something to reduce poverty?
Shabbat New York will date the World's Citizens Festival, an annual music event with a very impressive list of stars that will appear and an equally impressive goal of putting an end to poverty in the world.
The Citizens of the World Festival, which is taking place for the seventh time, will bring tens of thousands of people who will flock to a large lawn in Central Park not only to enjoy performances such as Janet Jackson, Cardy B and Sean Mendes, but also to increase awareness of the real goal of the event – to put an end to extreme poverty by 2030.
The World Citizens Film Festival in 2012 is a continuation of the World Poverty Project, an international support group that hopes to put an end to poverty by increasing the number of people actively fighting it.
To receive a free ticket to an event (unless you’re willing to pay for a VIP card), the concert attendees must complete a series of assignments, or “actions” such as volunteering, sending an e-mail to a global leader, or other significant ways to help increase awareness of the purpose of ending poverty.
But how much success did the festival really achieve, with just another 12 years ahead of it to fulfill its goal?
Is the idea of giving a free ticket to a concert to people really a way to convince them to demand a call-to-action, or is this another case of “clitivatism”—people who feel like they’re really having an impact by signing a petition on the Internet or sending a tweet?
“Since 2011, the Citizens of the World has documented more than 19 million “actions” from its supporters, pushing for a variety of different purposes.
According to him, these actions helped spur world leaders to declare commitments and policies equivalent to more than $37 billion that would affect the lives of more than 2.25 billion people by 2030.
At the beginning of 2018, the group cited 390 liabilities and declarations as a result of its operations, of which at least $10 billion had already began distribution or fundraising.
The group estimates that allocated funds have so far had a direct impact on nearly 649 million people around the world.
Some of the main commitments include The Power of Nutrition, a British investor-and-self-industrial partnership committed to “helping children grow to their full-to-purpotent,” promising to provide Rwanda’s $35 million to help put an end into the malnutrition in the country after receiving more than 4,700 tweets from “Citizens of the World.”
“With support from the UK government, donors, national governments and ‘citizens of the world’ like you, we can turn social justice on malnutrition into a footnotter in history,” said Ambassador The Power of Tune Tracy Ullman to the audience during the London Live Concert in April 2018.
The group also said that after more than 5,000 actions called on the UK to improve nutrition for mothers and children, the government has announced the allowance for the project, Power of Ten Nutrition, which will reach 5 million women and children with nutrition intervention.
In response to one of the frequently asked questions on their website, “What makes you think we can put an end to extreme poverty?”
‚ Citizens of the treasury of the treaps: „It will be a long and difficult route - sometimes we will fall and fail.
But, like the great movements before us of civil rights and resistance to apartheid, we will succeed, because we are stronger together.
