The inventor of the global Internet plans to start a new internet network to overcome Google and Facebook
Tim Berners-Lee, the inventor of the global Internet, is launching a start-up to compete with Facebook, Amazon and Google.
Inrupt, the man’s latest project that has become a legend in the world of technology, is a company that will open Solid, Berners-Lee’s open-source platform.
The Solid platform allows its users to choose where their data will be stored and the people who will be allowed to access the different data types.
In an exclusive interview with Fast Company, Berners-Lee joked that the intention behind Inrupt was “control of the world.”
“We have to do that now,” he said about the start-up.
“It’s a historic moment.”
The app uses Solid’s technology to enable people to create a “Personal Online Database” or POD.
The repository can include a list of contacts, lists of actions to perform, a schedule, a music library, and other personal and professional tools.
Imagine that Google Drive, Microsoft Outlook, Slack and Spotify will all be available in one browser, at the same time.
What distinguishes the personal online database is that only the user will decide who will be able to access the information and what type of information can be accessed.
The company calls it “personal empowerment through data.”
According to John Bruce, the company’s CEO, this means that Inrupt will find the right resources, process, and capabilities to help make Solid accessible to everyone.
The company currently includes Berners-Lee, Bruce, a security platform acquired by IBM, a team that includes a number of developers hired to work on the project, and a community of volunteer code programmers.
Starting this week, technology developers from around the world will be able to create decentralized apps using the tools available on the Inrupt website.
Berners-Lee said that he and his team aren’t discussing with “Facebook and Google about their agreement or their refusal to instill complete change, which will cause a complete makeové in all their business models.
“We don’t ask permission.”
“In a post published in Medium on Saturday, Berners-Lee wrote that Inrupt’s mission is to provide commercial energy and an environmental system to help protect the integrity and quality of the new network built in Solid.”
In 1994, Berners-Lee changed the face of the Internet when he founded the network’s standard organization, W3C at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology.
In recent months, Brenners-Lee has had an influential voice in the debate over network neutrality.
Also during Inrupt's launch, Berners-Lee will continue to serve as the founder and director of W3C, the Web Foundation, and Open Data Institute.
“I’m particularly optimistic about the network’s next era,” Berners-Lee added.
Barnard Van: An Event to commemorate the clergyman who won the Victoria Cross in World War I
The only clergyman from the Anglican Church who won, as a warrior of Victoria Cross during World War I, was noted at an event held in his hometown, 100 years after winning.
Vice-champion and revenant Bernard Vánn won the Victorian Cross on September 29, 1918 for his participation in an attack on Bellenglise and Lehaucourt.
However, he was killed by a sniper four days later and he was never informed that he had the highest British Medal of Excellency.
A commemorative stone was unveiled by his two grandchildren, at a parade held in Rashdan, in the Northern Humpton district, on Saturday.
One of his grandchildren, Michael Wane, said the exposing of the stone exactly 100 after his grandfather had won the Medal of Excellence is “remarkably symmbolic.”
On September 29, 1918, Lt. Coluplée van led his battalion and crossed the Saint-Kentan Canalyzer “through heavy fog and under heavy fire from field cannons and submachine guns.”
He then hurried to the line of fire and the “biggest chivalry” led the line forward before rushing to take over a submachine gun with one hand and dropped three of the company.
Lt. Column van was killed by a German sniper on October 4, 1918 - one month before the war ended.
The 72-year-old Michael Van said his grandfather’s actions were “something that I know I can never compare to but something that gives me humility.”
He and his brother, Dr. James Wan, also laid a wreath after the parade, led by the Royal Youth Orchestra of Brentwood.
Michael Wan said he “feels a great honor to take part in the march” and added “the bravery of a true protagonist is demonstrative in support given to many people.”
