Desert Island Discs: Tom Daly Sensing ‚Discomforsence Because His Sexual Inclination
Tom Daly, the Olympic water jumper, said he felt inferior from everyone else because of his sexual orientation—but that feeling motivated him to become a success.
The 24-year-old jumper said he didn’t knew until he got to middle school that “not everyone is like me.”
“In an interview with Desert Island Discs by Radio Channel 4, hosted by Lauren Laveren, he said he had spoken out about homosexual rights to give others “hope.”
He also said that becoming a parent caused him to worry less about winning the Olympic Games.
The regular moderator of the old program, Kristy Young, went on a few months of sick leave.
In his appearance as Levern’s first show, Daly said he felt “decommissance” from the others when he grew up because “it wasn’t socially acceptable to love boys and girls.”
“Until this day, the feeling of inferiority, and a sense of diversity, there have been real things that gave me the strength and the power and allowed me to succeed.”
He wanted to prove that “something,” he said, not disappointing everyone when they ended up discovering his sexual orientation.
The man who twice won the Olympic bronze medal became a high-profile leader of the LGBT community campaign and used his participation at the British Commonwealth Games in Australia this year to call on other countries to grant legal status to homosexuality.
He spoke openly on the subject because he felt lucky that he was given the opportunity to live openly and without consequences and wanted to give to others “hope.”
The winner of the World Championships said that infatuation with a man, American film creator Dustin Lance Black, whom he met in 2013, “caught me by surprise.”
Daly married the Oscar-winning winner, who is 20 years older, last year, but he said that the age gap has never been a problem.
„When you go through so much at such a young age” – he attended the Olympic Games for the first time at the age of 14 and his father died of cancer three years later – he said it was difficult to find someone at his age experiencing similar ups and downs.
The couple became parents in June, a son named Robert Ray Black-Daily, and Daley said his “whose whole point of view” had changed.
“If you’d asked me last year, it’s all revolved around ‚ I should win a gold medal,” he said.
„You know what, there are bigger things than Olympic gold medals.
“My Olympic gold medal is Robbie.”
His son was named after his father, Robert, who died in 2011 at the age of 40 after being diagnosed suffering from a cancerous brain tumor.
Daly said his father didn't accept the fact that he was about to die and that one of the last things he asked was whether they already had tickets to the Olympic Games in London 2012 - because he wanted to be in the front row.
“I couldn’t tell him, ‘I won’t be here to be in the front row, Father,” he said.
“I held his hand while his breathing stopped, and only when he really ceased breathing and I finally got to know that he wasn’t invincible,” he said.
A year later, Daley competed at the 2012 Olympic Games and won a bronze medal.
“I’ve just known I’ve been dreaming about this for the rest of my life—diving in front of a home audience at the Olympic Games, didn’t feel any better,” he said.
It’s also what inspired him to choose his first son-in-law’s Proud, with whom he identified during preparations for the Olympic Games and which still makes him goosebump.
Desert Island Discs is broadcast on BBC Radio 4 on Sunday at 11:15, according to British daylight saving time.
