Receission of Germany's new war ship has been postponed once again.
Germany's new naval friggitate was supposed to be accepted in 2014 to replace an agging war ships from the Cold War period, but it was postponed at least a year, due to defective and slugged-up systems, the local media reported.
Rheinland-Pfalz, the leading ship on the new Baden-Wuertemberg-class frittates, has now been postponed until the first half of 2019, according to a report by the newspaper Deight who quoted a military spokesperson.
The vessel was supposed to join the Navy in 2014, but worrying issues after the supply clipged on the ambitious project.
The fore four of the Baden-Wuerttemberg-class, which the navy commissioned back in 2007, will replace Bremen-class aging friggets.
It is expected that they will include a powerful cannon, anti-airplane and anti-ship missile batteries as well as evasive technologies, such as acoustic, infra-rejude and reduced radar signatures.
Other important features include longer maintenance periods - the newest frigates can be deployed for up to two years away from house ports.
However, the significance of repeated delays is that the innovative ships – which are supposed to allow Germany to send troops overseas – will already become obsolete by the moment they entered the service, noted enough.
F125-sourly frigata made headlines last year, when the German navy officially refused to accept the vessel and returned it to the Bloh & Voss shipyard in Hamburg.
It was the first time that the navy returned a ship to a ship builder after its delivery.
It’s not much is known about the reasons behind the return, but the German media cited a number of critical “software and hardware deficiencies” that made the warship useless to use a combat mission.
The software defects were especially important because Baden-Wuerttemberg-class navial tools will be operated by about 120 sailors—about half of the team used in the older Priggets, the Bremen class.
In addition, it has been discovered to have a dramatic overweight that reduces its performance and limits navy’s ability to add future upgrades.
Rheinland-Pfalz is 7,000 tons, weighing twice as much as ships used by the Germans in World War II.
Apart from the faulty software, the price label of the entire project – which also includes team coaching – is also becoming a problem.
It was rumored at a shocking height of €3.1 billion ($3.6 billion) – beyond the original €2.2 billion.
The problems that fascinate the newest friggets are becoming particularly important in light of recent warnings that Germany’s maritime force is shrinking.
Earlier this year this year, Hans-Peter Bartellas, the head of the German parliament’s security committee, authorized that the narrif�se “incompetent ships capable of deploying.”
The official source said the problem gained momentum and increased over time, as old ships were deprived but were not replaced by other vessels.
He expressed his regret that the navy could not be attached to the navy of any Baden-Wuerttemberg-class friggata.
Scotland's National Fund Listens to the Secret Life of Bats
A new study conducted at the mansion in the mountains of Scotland strives to reveal how bats sue the landscape to hunt their food.
The hope is that the findings will remark in a new way the behavior of unique flying mammals and will help guide conservation activities in the future.
The study conducted by scientists at the Scottish National Fund will follow common bats and European phones, as well as the long-ear-headed brown beanbonton batters in the Wester Ross Inner Gardens.
Special recording devices will be placed on key sites around the property to keep track of bat activities throughout the season.
The National Health Service (NHS) staff and volunteers will also conduct mobile surveys using manual detectors.
A specialized voice analysis of all recordings will determine the frequencies of bat calls and which of the species is responsible for them.
In the next step, the mertilient zone map will be drawn to create a detailed picture on the order of the landscape's surface, of their behavior patterns.
Rob-Dawar, a natural conservation consultant for the National Foundation of Scotland (NTS) hopes that the results will reveal which living areas are most important for bats and how each species uses them.
This information will help determine the benefits of the management work of the living zones, such as the creation of meadows and how to best maintain forest areas for bats and other related species.
Bat populations in Scotland and throughout the United Kingdom have decreased considerably during the last century.
They are threatened by development and construction work, which influencing the nesting and the loss of living areas.
Wind turbines and lighting may also pose a risk, and this is also true for fly traps and certain chemical treatments of building materials, as well as attacks from household cats.
In fact, bats are not blind.
However, due to their nail hunting habits, bat ears are more useful when it comes to catching prey.
They use a sophisticated technique of detection using echo to locate insects and identify obstacles to their flight trajectory.
The NTS Foundation, which is responsible for handling more than 270 historical buildings, 38 important gardens and 760,000 dunams of land throughout the country, takes bats very seriously.
It has 10 skilled specialists, who regularly conduct surveys, nesting tests and sometimes rescue.
The organization has also established the first and only reserve dedicated to bats at the Tarib Mansion in Damphries and Galaway, which serves as a home for eight of Scotland's ten bat species.
Mansion Director David Thompson said the mansion is the ideal territory for them.
“Here at T-Riv we have an excellent area for the bats,” he said.
We have old buildings, a lot of antiquity trees and all the right living areas.
But many things are not yet known about the bats, so the work we’re doing here and other assets will help us understand more about what they need to thrive.”
He emphasized the importance of the inspection to find the bats before carrying out maintenance work within the properties, because the unintentional destruction of a single populated nest may destroy up to 400 feminines and puppies, and may also destroy an entire local population.
Bats are protected sex and manslaughter, harassment and interruption of bats or the destruction of their nesting areas are prohibited by law.
Elizabeth Pearl, a Scotch Clerk at the Bat Conservation Fund, encouraged the public to shoulder and help.
She said: “We still have a lot to learn about our bats and many of our species we just don’t know the status of their populations.”
