Foreign buyers will be charged a higher stamp tax when buying properties in the UK
Foreign buyers will be taxed higher when buying properties in the UK and additional revenue will be used to help the homeless, according to the new Conservative program.
The move will neutralize the success of Corbyn's move to attract new voters
Shole Tax Accompreciation will be imposed on those who don’t pay taxes in the UK.
Treasury Expects Tax to Raise Up to £120 Million a Year to Help Homeless
Foreign buyers will be charged at a higher rate of stamp tax when they buy a property in the UK - additional revenue will be used to help the homeless, announcing Theresa May today.
The step seems to be an attempt to neutralize Jeremy Corbyn’s success in attracting young voters, with promises to provide cheaper housing and to focus on high-incomenants.
The stamp tax will be imposed on people and companies that do not pay taxes in the UK, and the addition of income will strengthen the government’s move to help those who sleep in difficult conditions.
The payment, which comes as an addition to the current stamp tax, includes higher tax levels on secondary housing and real estate investments for rental – may amount to three percent.
Treasury expects the move to raise up to £120 million a year.
It is estimated that 13% of the new assets built in London are purchased by foreign residents, a situation that causes a price increase and makes it difficult for people who first purchase a property to put a foot on the housing ladder.
Many rich regions in the country – especially in the capital – have become “ghost cities” because of the high number of foreign buyers who spend most of their time outside the country.
The new policy comes just a few weeks after Boris Johnson called for a stamp tax cut to help young people buy a first home.
He blamed large construction companies at the prices of the expensive properties, because they hijack lands but do not use it, and urged Ms. May to abandon quotas on homes at an affordable primity and fix Britain’s “housing disgrace.”
Korbyn announced a series of attractive reforms proposed in the field of housing, including supervision of rent and cessation of eviction “unculted.”
He also wants to give the councils greater power to build new homes.
“Last year I said that I would dedicate my term of office to the restoration of the British dream – the essence of which is that life is supposed to be better for every new generation.
And this means the correction of our broken housing market.
Britain will always be open to people who want to live, work and build a live here.
However, it’s unjust that people who don’t live in the UK, as well as companies based in foreign countries, will be able to buy homes with the same ease as British residents working hard.
The dream of becoming landlords has become too distant for too many people, and the humiliation of sleep in difficult conditions is too realistic.”
