Indonesia is located in the Pacific Fire Ring area, known for its seismic activity and is permanently damaged by earthquakes.
Here are a few earthquakes and tsunami waves from the last few years:
2004: A strong earthquake on the west coast of the Indonesian Indonesian province in northern Sumatra, on December 26, caused a tsunami wave that struck 14 states and killed 226,000 people along the Indian Ocean coastline, and more than half of those killed in Acha.
2005: A series of strong earthquakes hit the West Coast of Sumatra at the end of March and early April.
Hundreds of people died on the island of Nias, facing Sumatra Beach.
6.6: A 6.8-powered earthquake hit the south of Java, Indonesia’s most populated island and caused a tsunami wave that shattered on the southern coast and killed nearly 700 people.
209: A 7.6 magnitude earthquake struck an area near the city of Padang, the provincial capital “West Sumatra.”
More than 1,100 people were killed.
2010: A 7.5 magnitude earthquake struck one of the Mantaway Islands, opposite Sumatra, causing a tsunami wave up to 10 meters high, which destroyed dozens of villages and killed about 300 people.
2016: A superficial earthquake struck the province of Fiedi Jaia Bacha, causing destruction and horrible, because local people were recalled of the destruction after the 2004 earthquake and tsunami wave.
