Federal Prosecutores Asking a Rare Death Penalty to Suspect Terrorist Attack in New York City
U.S. federal plaintiffs in New York are seeking the death penalty for Saipolo Saipov, suspected of the terrorist attack in New York City that killed eight people—a rare-puntipulation that hasn't been carried out for a federal crime in the state since 1953.
The 30-year-old Saipov allegedly used a Hom Dippo-dried truck to carry out an attack on a bicyclist alongside the West Seide motorway in southern Manhattan, and ran over pedestrians and a cyclist who were on its way in October.
In order to justify the death penalty, prosecutors must prove that Saipov killed “intentionally” the eight victims and caused “intill intent” to serious injuries in the body, according to the Declaration of Intensitions for the Death Penalty, which was filed in the Southern District of New York.
According to the court's document, it is possible to demand the death penalty for these two indictments.
Weeks after the attack, a federal juriestman slaped Saipov with an indictment involving 22 counts, compremined with eight extortion-aibited murder charges, commonly used by federal prosecutors in organized crime cases, and charged with violence and deceiving motorized vehicles.
The attack demanded “significant planning in advance and intentionally at first,” the prosecutors said, describing the manner in which Saipov carried out the attack as “a slull, cruel and sick.”
“Saipolo Habibilovich Saipov caused harm, damage and loss to the families and friends of Diego Enrique Angelini, Nicholas Klebes, An-Lore Daked, Darren Drake, Ariel Arlige, Hernan Frucci, Hernan Diego Mendoza and Alejandro Damien Fápüko,” the statement said.
Five of the victims were tourists from Argentina.
The last the Southern Arrondissement of the New York City suspended the death penalty in the case occurred a decade ago.
The defendant, Khalid Barnes, was convicted of the murder of two drug dealers, but was eventually sentenced to life imprisonment in September 2009.
The last the Death Penalty in New York City was in 1953 for Julius and Ethel Rosenberg, a married couple who had been executed after convincing conspiring for espionage for the Soviet Union during the Cold War two years earlier.
The two Rosenbergs were executed in an electric chair on June 19, 1953.
According to court documents, Saipov, born in Uzbekistan, did not express feelings of remorse in the days and months after the attack.
He stated to the interrogators that he felt good about his actions, the police said.
Saipov told the authorities that he had drawn inspiration for the attack after watching Daash videos on his phone, according to the indictment.
According to the police, he also asked to hang a ISIS flag in the hospital room where he was staying.
He claimed that he was innocent in response to the 22-party indictment.
David Patton, one of the federal defendants representing Saipov, said they were “disapprehened, of course” from the prosecution’s decision.
“We think that the decision to apply for the death penalty instead to make an admission of guilt that will lead a life sentence without the possibility of liberation will only prodigate the traum duration of these events on all those involved,” Patton said.
Saipov’s defense team has previously asked the plaintiffs not to demand the death penalty.
