Bubbling women openly on a bridge to prevent suicidies
Two women who have lost loved ones who have committed suicide are working to prevent others from taking their lives.
Sharon Davis and Kelly Humphries hung postcards on Wal-shorch bridge with inspirational messages and phone numbers that people can call to get support.
Ms. Davies' son, Tyler, was 13 years old when he began to suffer from depression and committed suicide at the age of 18.
“I don’t want any other parents to feel the way I feel every day,” she said.
Davies, the 45-year-old, who lived in Leydney, said her son was a promising chef with a contagious smile.
“Everybody recognized him for his smile.
“They always said his smile lit up every room.”
But he stopped working before his death, because there was “in a really dark place.”
In 2014, Tyler's brother, who was 11 at the time, found his brother after committing suicide.
Ms. Davis said: “I’m constantly afraid of a chain reaction.”
Ms. Davis created the postcards, “so that people will know that there is someone you can turn and talk to, even if it’s a friend.
“Don’t sit in silence – you need to talk.”
Ms. Humphreys, Ms. Davis’s friend for years, has lost Mark, a life partner for 15 years, shortly after his mother’s death.
“He didn’t say he was feeling depressed or depressed or anything else,” she said.
„A few days before Christmas, we noticed a change in his attitude.
“When the children opened the gifts, he didn’t make eye contact with them or anything.”
She said that his death was a huge trauma for them, but they overcame her: “It creates a hole in the family.
It's tearing us apart.
“We all have to keep fighting.”
