Thow U.S.-China Trade War Could Benefit Beijing
The opening volley of the U.S.-China trade war was deafening, and although the battle is far away from it, a rift between the countries may be beneficial to Beijing in the long trench, experts say.
U.S. President-President Donald Trump shot earlier this year this yea has imposed a tax on China’s important export products, including solar collectors, steel and aluminum.
The most significant escalation took place this week with new tariffs that affected $200 billion (££150 million), effectively imposing taxes on half of commodities imported to the U.S. from China.
Beijing reacted the same correcie each time, and recently raised tariffs by five to ten percent on U.S. commodities worth $60 billion.
China has pledged a blow to every U.S. strike, and it is unlikely that the world’s second-largest economy will surrender in the near future.
A request from Washington to retrigue means subsibilization of its demands, but a public bow to the U.S. will be too embarrassing for Chinese President Xi Jinping.
However, experts say that if Beijing manages to conduct itself correctly, pressure from the U.S. trade war may positively support China in the lundrent term, reducing interdependence between the two economies.
“The fact that a quick political decision in Washington or Beijing could create conditions for the beginning of economic disintegration at each of the countries is in fact much more dangerous than what India has watched in the past,” said Abigail Grace, a research co-coordinating partner in Asia, the New American Security thinking team.
