Update on Red tides: Concentrations are decliding in Pinelas, Manatí and Sarasota
The latest report from the Florida Wild and Fish Life Commission presents a general decline in red tide concentrations in part of the Tampa Bay area.
According to the FWC Committee, more dispersed rash conditions have been reported in the provinces of Pinalas, Manati, Sarasota, Charlotte and Collière, which indicates diminishing concentrations.
The blossom of red tide extends over about 130 miles of the coastline, from the northern province of Pinelas to the southern Li district.
The plots can be found about 10 miles in front of Hillsboro County's coast, but in a smaller number of locations compared to last week.
Red tige was also observed in Pesco County.
Medium concentrations on the coastline or off the coast of the Pinellas province were reported last week, low-to-medium concentrations against the coastline of Hillsboro Obensity, background concentrations up to high-concentrations in Manatai province, background concentrations up to high concentrations on the coastline or off the coast of Sarasota County, background concentrations up to medium-sized concentrations in the Charlotte province, background concentrations up to high concentrations on the coastline or opposite the coast of Li Province.
Respiratory reports of respiratory stimuli from the provinces of Finlas, Manati, Sarasota, Lee and Collier are ongoing.
There have been not reported irritation in the northwestern Florida during the past week.
